= Abstract

With the continuous digital transformation of campus learning spaces, library seat management is gradually shifting from manual inspection and informal occupation to online reservation, real-time sensing, and rule-based coordination. To address practical issues such as poor visibility of available seats, weak arrival constraints after reservation, and inconsistency between displayed seat status and actual on-site conditions, this thesis rewrites the project draft around the real implementation in the current repository and presents a more complete preliminary thesis for an intelligent self-study seat reservation system.

The system adopts a layered structure composed of a mini-program client, backend service modules, and sensing devices. The backend is built with NestJS and integrates TypeORM, MySQL, and Redis for data modeling, persistence, and reservation locking. The reservation module handles creation, cancellation, expiration, and history queries; the check-in module supports QR-code and location-based verification; the seat module maintains seat queries, status transitions, and status logs; the statistics module provides date-range statistics, area heatmap queries, and daily aggregation; WebSocket events are used for seat status synchronization and reservation notifications; and MQTT is used to ingest device reports from ESP32 nodes equipped with HC-SR501 infrared sensors. On the client side, the WeChat mini-program developed with Taro and React provides seat browsing, reservation, check-in, history viewing, and credit score related entries.

According to the current codebase, the implemented prototype has already completed several key paths, including a 30-minute reservation holding period, automatic expiration release for pending reservations, basic check-in verification, credit score deduction for no-shows, seat status logging, device-side sensor reporting, and real-time seat status synchronization on the mini-program. However, some abilities remain incomplete or only partially prepared, such as automatic release after a long absence, a fully implemented favorites feature, mature user portrait statistics, a complete operational analytics system, fully deployed reminder pushing, and an admin console. Therefore, this thesis describes them conservatively instead of presenting them as completed production functions.

Rather than expanding beyond the real project boundary, this draft is organized strictly around the modules, pages, services, entities, and tests that already exist in the repository. It aims to provide a more formal and structurally complete thesis foundation that can be refined further in subsequent iterations.

Key Words: library; seat reservation; WeChat mini-program; NestJS; MQTT; WebSocket

